zed_order":1,"number":null,"original_order":1,"raw_file_hash":"sha256:ac32f24fb8305f73fc2215f7f976639325d72905dd789c2560dc000df5c5cd9b","source_file_key":"sha256:1ddf44f33e1247e5316256c8fa6bdfa0a72822e8d01adafb348db829357a1f7d","source_key":"body","title":"9.1: How to Calculate the Force Output of an Hydraulic Cylinder in Extention (Pushing)","unit_id":"unit:2010dc988f8178a883a17b391618d24c29eadb2bef63efb4d17f8139f9a49ff3","volume":""}],"version":"corpus-integrity.v2","work_completion":"UNKNOWN"},"parser_hash":"sha256:0a337e55a419b48b7ad67c1a573dc986a152d8e45524c9b4a347b865c52fa955","parser_profile":{"completeness":"EXCERPT","constants":{"author":"Jessy Haid / Northeast Wisconsin Technical College","title":"9.1: How to Calculate the Force Output of an Hydraulic Cylinder in Extention (Pushing)","work_id":"hydraulics-04"},"encoding":"utf-8","expected_units":1,"fields":{"text":"text"},"filters":{},"format":"html","html_body_selector":".mt-content-container","html_remove_selectors":["script","style","nav","header","footer","noscript"],"layout":"SINGLE_WORK","normalization":{"boundaries":[],"candidate_threshold":0.8,"duplicate_body_min_chars":128,"expected_toc":[],"external_work_ids":[],"heading_max_chars":100,"max_unit_bytes":2000000,"min_body_chars":80,"segmentation":"EXPLICIT","toc_evidence":"","unicode_form":"NFC","version":"chinese-longform.v2","work_completion":"UNKNOWN"},"records_path":"item","version":"corpus-parser.v3","work_key_encoding":"VALUE"},"schema_version":"corpus-parsed-work.v1","title":"hydraulics-04","units":[{"author":"Jessy Haid / Northeast Wisconsin Technical College","body_content_hash":"sha256:2b5d003ca01d3e4530b4917856fc13591c1d67f91c2717de244cf6e114080f2d","chapter_id":"unit:2010dc988f8178a883a17b391618d24c29eadb2bef63efb4d17f8139f9a49ff3","confirmed_heading_chars":0,"edition_id":"source-edition","normalization_evidence":{"normalized_unicode_length":2161,"normalized_utf8_bytes":2197,"precision":"STRUCTURED_LOCATOR","raw_text_changed":false,"steps":["STRICT_DECODE","NEWLINE_LF","NFC"]},"normalized_content_hash":"sha256:c41177dfa230a7416de47f3ab97bafdb6ce8f8813e777e28fefe4297dd8a9188","order":1,"original_content_hash":"sha256:c41177dfa230a7416de47f3ab97bafdb6ce8f8813e777e28fefe4297dd8a9188","parser":{"profile_hash":"sha256:0a337e55a419b48b7ad67c1a573dc986a152d8e45524c9b4a347b865c52fa955"},"physical_order":1,"raw_blob_id":"63b13391-2eb7-5aea-a90a-db4bd691bfb4","raw_file_hash":"sha256:ac32f24fb8305f73fc2215f7f976639325d72905dd789c2560dc000df5c5cd9b","source_chapter_id":"body","source_locator":{"container_path":null,"field":"text","offset_kind":"HTML_SELECTOR","original_byte_offset":null,"path":"hydraulics-04.html","record":1,"selector":".mt-content-container"},"text":"Figure \\(\\PageIndex{1}\\): Diagram of a hydraulic cylinder in extension.\nThe output force of a hydraulic cylinder depends on two factors:\nThe\npressure\nsupplied by the hydraulic system\nThe\narea\nover which that pressure is applied\nWhen a cylinder is extending, fluid pressure acts across the full\npiston area\non the cap end of the cylinder. The force produced is the product of pressure and this piston area.\nTo calculate theoretical force output:\nForce (lbs)=Pressure (psi) × Piston Area (in²)\nWhere the piston area is calculated by:\nA = 0.7854 × D²\nThis formula assumes no losses due to friction or leakage. In practice, the actual force may be slightly lower due to these factors, but this gives a reliable baseline for sizing components and understanding system capabilities.\nExample: Calculating the Extension Force of a Cylinder Given its Size and Pressure\nTo determine the force output of a hydraulic cylinder when it's extending, use the following formula:\nP × A = F\nWhere:\nF\n= Force in pounds (lbs)\nP\n= Pressure in pounds per square inch (psi)\nA\n= Area of the piston in square inches (in²)\nSince hydraulic cylinders use round pistons, we calculate piston area using the formula for the area of a circle:\nA = 0.7854 ×\nD\n²\nWhere\nD\nis the bore diameter of the cylinder.\nExample Calculation\nLet’s say the cylinder has a\nbore diameter of 2.0 inches\nand is subjected to\n1000 psi\nof hydraulic pressure.\nA=0.7854 ×\nD\n²\nA= 0.7854 × (2.0)²\nA = 0.7854 × 4 = 3.1416 in.²\nF = P × A\nF=1000 × 3.1416=3,142 lbs\nResult:\nThe cylinder can theoretically exert\n3,142 pounds of force\nwhile extending at 1000 psi.\nAdditional Practice Problem\nDetermine the minimum pressure required to lift a known load given:\nLoad to lift = 5,000 lbs\nCylinder Bore = 3.0 in\nRod Diameter = 1.0 in\nWe’re lifting, so this is an extension force problem. First, calculate piston area:\nA = 0.7854 × (3.0)² = 0.7854 × 9 = 7.069 in²\nNow solve for pressure:\nP = F / A = 5000 x 7.069 ≈ 707 psi\nAnswer\nA pressure of approximately\n707 psi\nis required to lift a 5,000 lb load using a 3-inch bore cylinder.\nA\nbig lesson\nto learn here is that\nlarger bore cylinders\nreduce the pressure\nrequired\nto move a load.","title":"9.1: How to Calculate the Force Output of an Hydraulic Cylinder in Extention (Pushing)","volume":"","work_id":"hydraulics-04","work_title":"hydraulics-04"}],"work_id":"hydraulics-04"}